# .rules

The code rules of this repo. Read this before writing or changing C++ and write the code so that the
format and lint hooks find nothing to report: a lint run is slow (clang-tidy over every file), a failed one
costs a full re-run, and most failures are things these rules already name. `CLAUDE.md` keeps the commands and
a pointer here; every rule lives in this file, the workflow (section 7) as well as the code.

Where a rule comes from is in brackets: [CLAUDE.md] is a rule stated there before this file existed,
[.clang-format] and [.clang-tidy] are checks the hooks enforce (the config is the source of truth),
[seen] is a failure that actually happened in this repo, and [probed] is a check confirmed by running
`clang-tidy` on a scratch file that breaks it (recursion, unused parameter, uninitialized local, `int` as a
condition, `return true / false`, `.size() == 0`, `int` -> `float`).

## 1. Layering and structure (hard rules)

How the code is put together (the layers, the document model, undo, the `.pxa` format) is described in
`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`; read the section before changing that part. This file keeps only what must not be
done there:

- **Layering** (`#layers`): CMake enforces the order core -> io -> ui. Never include SDL or ImGui from
  `src/core` or `src/io`. Public headers live in `include/tinysprite/` (core and io only), internal ones
  next to sources. [CLAUDE.md]
- **Document model** (`#document-model`): do not flatten `Sprite -> Layers x Frames -> Cel`. [CLAUDE.md]
- **Undo/Redo** (`#editing-and-undo`): every edit goes through the history module, one undo step per user
  stroke. Do not mutate cels outside this path. [CLAUDE.md]
- **File format** (`#file-format`): `.pxa` is versioned and chunked (length-prefixed). [CLAUDE.md]
- **A group of four or more files with the same prefix in one `src/` folder lives in a subfolder of that
  name.** Count every file (a `.cpp` and its `.hpp` are two); the prefix is the part of the name before
  the first `_`. Examples: `src/ui/app/`, `src/ui/editor/`, `src/ui/tilemap/`, `src/ui/file/`,
  `src/ui/layer/`, `src/core/tools/`. Move the whole group in the change that adds the fourth file, with
  `git mv`; update the includes (`"tilemap/tilemap_panel.hpp"`, relative to `src/ui`, which is on the
  include path), the source list in `src/ui/CMakeLists.txt` (`src/core` and `src/io` are globbed), and
  re-run cmake. The tests stay flat in `tests/` and `tests/ui/`. `layers_panel` has the prefix `layers`,
  not `layer`, so it stays where it is.
- **Keep directories shallow: `src/<layer>/<group>/` is the deepest level** (`src/ui/app/`,
  `src/core/tools/`). Never create a folder inside a group folder and never a group folder for fewer than
  four files; a group that keeps growing is split by renaming its prefix into two groups at the same
  level, not by nesting.
- Everything drawn in pixels goes through `theme::px()`, every color is a `theme.hpp` token, every shared
  widget is a `theme::` helper (`DESIGN.md`).
- Do not add a data member to `App` (`src/ui/app.hpp`) for state that belongs to a module: it is at the edge
  of the padding check (section 5). Put the state where it is owned (for the UI scale, `theme`). [seen]

## 2. Tests

- **TDD**: write the failing test first (`tests/test_<feature>.cpp`, one file per feature, using
  `tests/minitest.hpp`: `TEST`, `RUN`, `ASSERT_*`), then implement. Watch it fail for the right reason
  (a compile error for a missing function counts), then make it pass. [CLAUDE.md]
- **No commit changes behavior without its test, whoever wrote the change** (a fix, a feature, someone's
  work in progress staged for a commit). The test comes first and is run red: against the code without the change
  it fails for the reason the change removes. A change found without a test gets one before it is committed, and
  writing it often finds the change is wrong (a popup was checked after the menu bar had closed it). [seen]
- **A refactoring needs a net that catches a break.** "The tests pass" is no proof: break the code you
  changed on purpose (flip a condition, drop a guard), run the tests, and see them fail. A break that survives
  means the behavior is not covered: write the characterization test first. A break nobody can see (an
  equivalent mutant) is noted as such. [seen]
- UI tests are `tests/ui/ui_<feature>.cpp` under `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy`; layout and golden pixel arrays:
  `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md#testing`. [CLAUDE.md]
- New `test_*.cpp` files are picked up by CMake globbing (re-run cmake). [CLAUDE.md]

## 3. Code conventions

- C++17, no raw owning pointers: document types are RAII classes; live strokes are created by a static
  `begin()` returning a `std::unique_ptr` (null if the history already has an open stroke) and cancel
  themselves when destroyed unfinished. Invalid sizes throw `std::invalid_argument`; refusable operations
  return `bool` or null. Details: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`. Tests run under ASan/UBSan. [CLAUDE.md]
- Keep every function in `src/` at a cognitive complexity of 15 or less (not applied to `tests/`). Split by
  what the branches decide, not by line count (examples: `pxa.cpp`'s `Decoder`, `timeline_panel.cpp`'s
  `grid`). [CLAUDE.md] [.clang-tidy]
- A library is declared in `vcpkg.json`, never copied into the repo; record its name, version and license in
  `thirdparty/README.md`. A font or other asset kept in `thirdparty/` also has its license beside it. [CLAUDE.md]
- Strings in the UI are English, sentence case, no trailing period on labels (`DESIGN.md`).

**Comments** follow chapter 8 of the Linux kernel coding style
(https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/v4.10/process/coding-style.html), adapted to C++. They are explicit and
clean, and they explain what a piece of code is for and how its parts fit, never narrate its steps.
- A comment says **what** the code does and, when it is not obvious, **why**. Never explain **how** it
  works: make the code obvious instead (a good name, a smaller function). A comment that explains bad code
  is wasted; fix the code.
- Put the comment **at the head of the function** (or class, or file): what it does, what it takes and
  returns or changes, what it refuses, and why it exists. Avoid comments inside a function body: if a body
  needs its parts explained, it is too complex (section 3, cognitive complexity), so split it. A short
  comment inside to warn about something clever or ugly is fine; do not add more than that.
- **Comment data.** One declaration per line, and a short comment on each member or constant that says
  what it is for (units, range, who owns it, what a special value means). `float uiScale = 0.0f; /* 0 =
  none */`-style trailing notes are fine for short ones; a longer note goes on the lines above.
- Multi-line comments use this form, the `/*` and the `*/` each alone on a line and a column of `*`:

      /*
       * What the function does, in a sentence or two,
       * and why it does it.
       */

  Single-line comments use `/* ... */`. `//` is only for lint markers (`// NOLINT(...)`).
- **A comment is checked against the code it describes.** When you change code, re-read the comments on it; before you write a fact
  into one (a limit, a lifetime, who calls it) open the declaration or grep the use. Two fixes were titled "comments that did not
  match the code". [seen]
- This applies to every comment you add or touch. Existing comments (most start their text on the `/*`
  line) are not rewritten in bulk.

## 4. Formatting [.clang-format]

Write code the way `clang-format` would, so the hook has nothing to change (a hook that modifies a file
fails the run and needs a second one). LLVM style with these settings:

- 4 spaces, no tabs, at most 100 columns (a comment counts), Linux braces (a function's `{` on its own
  line, every other `{` on the line of its `if` / `for` / `class`).
- `AllowShort*: None`: a function, `if`, loop or block is never on one line. Always braces and line breaks.
- `case` labels are not indented inside `switch`. Pointers bind right: `int *p`, `const char *name`.
- Includes sorted case-sensitively within a block. A trailing comment on consecutive lines is aligned by the
  formatter: do not align by hand, and expect a new longer line to re-align its neighbours.
- A class member declaration with a comment that pushes it past 100 columns is wrapped by the formatter in
  an ugly way: put the comment above the member.

## 5. What clang-tidy rejects [.clang-tidy]

Warnings are errors. Enabled: `bugprone-*`, `clang-analyzer-*`, `misc-*`, `modernize-*`, `performance-*`,
`readability-*` and four `cppcoreguidelines-*` checks. Write the code so none of these fires:

**Declarations**
- Initialize every local at its declaration: `int count = 0;`, never `int count;` (init-variables).
- Initialize members in the declaration or the initializer list, not in the constructor body
  (prefer-member-initializer). A class that declares a destructor, copy or move operation declares all five
  or none (special-member-functions). An rvalue-reference parameter is moved from or not taken as `&&`.
- A global that is not `const` needs `// NOLINT(cppcoreguidelines-avoid-non-const-global-variables)` and a
  reason in the comment above it (see `theme.cpp`).
- No recursion (misc-no-recursion): walk with an explicit stack or a loop.
- A member function that does not change the object is `const` (readability-make-member-function-const).
  A parameter that is never used has no name or is left out (misc-unused-parameters): do not write
  `(void)x;`, remove the parameter. [probed]
- A class with many data members has a padding budget (clang-analyzer-optin.performance.Padding): order
  members largest first (pointers and containers, then 8- and 4-byte values, then `bool` and `char`), and
  put a new `float` next to the other `float`, not between `bool`s. `App` is at the limit. [seen]

**Expressions**
- Braces on every `if`, `else`, `for`, `while`, even for one statement (braces-around-statements).
- No `else` after `return`, `break`, `continue` (else-after-return). Test emptiness with `.empty()`, not
  `.size() == 0` (container-size-empty). Return the condition, not `if (c) { return true; } return false;`
  (simplify-boolean-expr).
- A pointer, a smart pointer or a `std::optional` may be used as a condition; an `int`, `float` or
  `size_t` is compared explicitly: `if (count != 0)`, `if (!(longest > 0.0f))`. A `bool` is not an `int`
  either: `(flag ? 1 : 0)`, never `flag + other` (implicit-bool-conversion).
- No implicit narrowing: `int` <-> `float`, `size_t` -> `int`, `double` -> `float` need a `static_cast`
  (bugprone-narrowing-conversions). Widen before multiplying sizes:
  `static_cast<size_t>(w) * static_cast<size_t>(h)` (implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result).
- Two branches with the same body are one branch (bugprone-branch-clone). Use `auto *` for a pointer
  (qualified-auto), `nullptr` for a null pointer, `using` over `typedef`, `emplace_back` over
  `push_back(T(...))`, a range `for` over an index loop that only reads, `= default` for a trivial
  special member (`modernize-*`).

**Performance**
- Take a non-trivial type by `const &` unless the function keeps a copy, and then take it by value and
  `std::move` it (performance-unnecessary-value-param). A range `for` over class objects binds `const auto &`
  (performance-for-range-copy). Do not `std::move` a `const` object or a trivially copyable one.

**Length**
- Cognitive complexity 15 per function (section 3). When a function grows past it, split out what a branch
  decides (a predicate, a helper per case), not the line count.

## 6. Order of work that keeps lint cheap

1. Write the test and the code to this file; do not leave style to the hooks.
2. While iterating, format and tidy only the changed files:
   `pre-commit run clang-format --files <files>` then
   `pre-commit run clang-tidy-full --hook-stage manual --files <files>` (needs `build/compile_commands.json`).
3. Once, when all the work is done, the full set (`pre-commit run --all-files --hook-stage manual > log`).
   Its exit code is 1 when the formatter changed a file even if nothing else failed: run it again and judge
   the second run. Read the diagnostics with `grep -E '(error|warning): ' log | grep -v 'warnings generated'`.
4. A commit hook that reformats a file stops the commit: `git add` the file and commit again.

## 7. Workflow and commits (mandatory) [CLAUDE.md]

**Commits**
- English only, following Conventional Commits (https://www.conventionalcommits.org/ko/v1.0.0/), e.g. `feat(core): add flood fill`.
- **One commit, one purpose.** A subject that needs three commas (`fix: guard the ellipse span, UTF-8 paths, GIF range, ...`) is
  several commits; split them so one can be reverted alone. Formatting sweeps, moves and behavior changes are never in the same
  commit. [seen]
- **The subject says what changed**, in about 72 characters. "address review of ...", "resolve bugs found in a code review" and "act
  on the audit findings" say nothing: name the fixes (one commit each). Two commits never share a subject. [seen]
- **Look before you add.** Run `git status --short` and add files by name, never `git add -A` / `git add .`: a binary, a log or an
  editor file (`pitch.pxa`, 65 KB) once went into a UI fix and stayed in the history. A file that does not belong to the change is
  left out, and a new kind of local file goes into `.gitignore` in its own commit. [seen]
- **Check before the commit, not after CI.** Format and tidy the changed files and build the tests first (section 6). A follow-up
  commit named "satisfy clang-tidy" or "clear the findings CI reported" means this step was skipped; it happened at least seven
  times. [seen]
- **CI builds on Linux with GCC and ASan/UBSan** and a `switch` over an enum must name every enumerator (`-Werror=switch`). A lambda
  or callback that outlives the function never captures a local by reference (stack-use-after-return). A test closes its streams
  before it removes the folder. A change that only built on Windows is not checked. [seen]
- **A rule applies where it says it applies.** Read the scope before moving files for it: the folder-per-prefix rule is for `src/`,
  not `tests/`; moving the tests and moving them back cost two commits. [seen]

These rules exist because they were broken repeatedly; follow them without being reminded.

**Build folder**
- **One build folder: `build/`.** Never create `build-fast/`, `build-release/`, `build-asan/` or any other `build-*` folder, and never point a
  preset or a script at one. clang-tidy, clangd and the pre-commit hooks read `build/compile_commands.json`, so a second folder leaves them
  looking at a stale one. To change the configuration (build type, sanitizers, UI on or off), run `cmake --preset <name>` again in `build/`;
  after a change of generator, delete `build/` first. The `dev` preset (Debug, Ninja, lld, ccache, no sanitizers) is the default; add
  `-DTINYSPRITE_SANITIZE=ON` to match the Linux CI job.
- A comparison of two configurations (a benchmark) builds in the scratchpad or another folder outside the repo, and deletes it afterwards.

**Lint and format**
- Run the hooks (`pre-commit run --all-files --hook-stage manual`, or the clang-tidy hook on the changed files while iterating) before every commit that touches C++, and fix everything they report. Run the full set before opening or updating a PR.
- Judge a check by its real exit code. Never pipe it through `tail`/`head` and read `$?` (that is the pipe's status): redirect to a log file, then test `$?` and read the log.
- CI runs the same pinned hooks, but it is the source of truth (it lints with the UI build, a stale local `build/` may not). If CI lint fails and the local run is clean, change the code so the flagged construct is gone (do not assume the first fix worked), then watch CI again.
- Never report "lint passed" or "tests passed" without having checked the exit code of that run.

**Branches and PRs**
- Small, low-risk changes (docs, typos, comments, scripts and tooling, small fixes, test-only changes, chores) are committed directly to `main` and pushed. No branch, no PR.
- Use a feature branch plus PR only for large features or risky changes (new modules or milestones, file format changes, behavior changes across layers, anything hard to revert). Name it `feat/...` or `fix/...`.
- Do not create a branch "by default" for every change.

**Merging a PR** (in this order, never skip a step)
1. Review the diff (read it; run `/code-review` for non-trivial changes) and fix findings.
2. Wait for CI: `gh pr checks <n> --watch`. The watch can end with "no checks reported" before CI registers: re-check until the jobs really ran. Merge only when every job passed.
3. Merge with `gh pr merge <n> --merge --delete-branch`, then `git checkout main && git pull --ff-only` and `git branch -d <branch>` for the local copy. A merged branch is never left behind, locally or on the remote.
4. If CI on `main` turns red after a merge, fixing it comes before any new work.
